<BaseLayout title="Product Workshop — Matthew Cochran" description="A disciplined product-development workshop for turning ambiguous opportunities into executable, verifiable product systems.">
  <PageHeader grid eyebrow="Protocol: WP-01 / Type: Methodology" title="The Product Workshop" description="An integrated framework for industrial-grade systems. Moving from observation to verified artifacts through deterministic project discipline and governed AI engineering." aside="Observe & Frame → Decompose & Plan → Design & Implement → Verify & Maintain" />
  <section class="section shell workshop-intro"><div><p class="kicker">Working premise</p><h2>Ambiguity should decrease at every stage.</h2></div><div><p>The workshop makes product, project, and technical assumptions visible before they become expensive. Each phase produces durable artifacts that constrain the next: evidence, boundaries, dependency models, executable plans, and verification records.</p></div></section>
  <section class="workshop-protocol shell">
    <aside><nav><a href="#observe"><span>Phase 01</span>Observe &amp; Frame</a><a href="#plan"><span>Phase 02</span>Decompose &amp; Plan</a><a href="#build"><span>Phase 03</span>Design &amp; Implement</a><a href="#verify"><span>Phase 04</span>Verify &amp; Maintain</a></nav></aside>
    <div class="protocol-phases">
      <article id="observe"><span>01 / Market &amp; Intent</span><h2>The Foundation of Utility</h2><p>Every product begins with a cold-eyed assessment of market and user needs. Jobs-to-be-Done, product boundaries, disconfirming evidence, and failure analysis establish where model judgment creates utility and where deterministic logic must retain authority.</p><div class="phase-artifact"><strong>Inputs</strong><ul><li>Stakeholder elicitation</li><li>ICP resonance mapping</li><li>FMECA and trust boundaries</li><li>Go / change / stop decision</li></ul></div></article>
      <article id="plan"><span>02 / Planning Discipline</span><h2>Deterministic Execution</h2><p>Complexity is managed through decomposition. Critical Path Method models dependencies, slack, bottlenecks, and lock-aware parallel work so both human and agentic collaborators operate against an executable plan.</p><div class="phase-artifact"><strong>Decision artifacts</strong><ul><li>CPM network and milestones</li><li>Risk-first increments</li><li>Acceptance criteria</li><li>Human and agent responsibilities</li></ul></div></article>
      <article id="build"><span>03 / Technical Construction</span><h2>Architecture as Strategy</h2><p>Architecture is designed with explicit failure boundaries, versioned contracts, modular kernels, and policy-gated state machines. Model reasoning is separated from authoritative tool execution.</p><div class="phase-artifact"><strong>Construction controls</strong><ul><li>Architecture and interfaces</li><li>Model/tool contracts</li><li>Human approval gates</li><li>Operational observability</li></ul></div></article>
      <article id="verify"><span>04 / Verification &amp; Soundness</span><h2>Earning Trust</h2><p>The pipeline concludes with evidence: product validation, threat models, test results, model evaluations, release readiness, and continuous observation of quality, latency, cost, and drift.</p><div class="phase-artifact verified-artifact"><strong>Verification artifacts</strong><ul><li>FMECA and security evidence</li><li>CI and evaluation records</li><li>Release readiness</li><li>Learning loop</li></ul></div></article>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="methodology"><div class="method-grid"></div><div class="shell method-content"><p class="kicker">Applied tooling</p><h2>The method is becoming software.</h2><p>Praxec cognitive architectures and deterministic MCP tools—including CPM planning, elicitation, FMECA, cross-matrix analysis, and scientific process—bring the same discipline into agentic engineering workflows.</p><a class="button primary" href="https://github.com/praxec" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Explore Praxec ↗</a></div></section>
</BaseLayout>
